      "thought": "Disassembly from arbitrary offsets is unreliable. Let me try a completely different approach — use the SeaBIOS debug symbols if available. Does Ubuntu ship the seabios debug symbols? Or better: check whether the running QEMU is actually using /usr/share/seabios/bios.bin in the first place!\n\nWait. Hold on. Let me double-check. The QEMU package here — /usr/bin/qemu-system-i386 from qemu-system-x86. The BIOS location: on Debian/Ubuntu, does qemu-system-i386 look for the BIOS in /usr/share/seabios/? QEMU's default BIOS path is compiled in: Debian's QEMU uses the \"seabios\" firmware from /usr/share/seabios (patched). Let me verify with QEMU's `-d` log, or check the firmware with `qemu-system-i386 -machine pc -d guest_errors`… Actually, the easiest way: run `QEMU_FW_DIR`? No, wrong. Let me check the compiled-in path: `strings /usr/bin/qemu-system-i386 | grep -i bios`.\n\nMore importantly — maybe the problem isn't the BIOS. Let me reconsider: EIP=0x10D01 is frozen, no serial output.\n\nHmm, wait, actually — let me reconsider the whole situation. SeaBIOS outputs to serial by default. Zero serial output = SeaBIOS hasn't even initialized the serial… or the serial chardev isn't working.\n\nWait, is my `-serial file:/app/serial.log` actually connected? Let me test: QEMU's `-serial file:` — data from the guest gets written to the file. SeaBIOS's early boot does write to serial (the \"iPXE\" line? No, that's iPXE; SeaBIOS's POST messages like \"Booting from Hard Disk\" go to both serial and VGA).\n\nIf the screen is black AND serial is empty… SeaBIOS is stuck before the first output. The first thing SeaBIOS does after reset: `boot_init`… the very first serial output happens during `serial_init` (the \"SeaBIOS (version...)\" banner is emitted via `romprintf`… actually the banner is emitted at an early stage of `rom_init`/`main`).\n\nHmm, so we're stuck in very early initialization. Early initialization includes:\n- `pit_setup`\n- `serial_init` (probes serial p