      "thought": "Very interesting:\n\n1. The window from file 0x10000 (40 bytes: `eb ef 66 8b b1 14 28 ...`) is found at guest 0xE9F0A. So part of the image is at 0xE9F0A (within the 0xE0000 region) — this is consistent with the image being placed at 0xE0000 (0xE9F0A - 0xE0000 = 0x9F0A; hmm, file 0x10000 → guest 0xE9F0A → base = 0xE9F0A - 0x10000 = 0xD9F0A).\n\n2. The code window at guest 0x10000 (8b 1c 0d 66 8b b1...) doesn't exist anywhere in the file. The code at guest 0x10D00 doesn't exist anywhere in the file either.\n\n3. The window of the stuck code region from file 0x10CF0 doesn't exist in the guest.\n\nSo: the code at guest 0x10000-0x10D10 is not from bios.bin. It was written there at runtime. By whom?\n\nSeaBIOS copies the 16-bit real-mode trampoline code into the low memory region at runtime! Yes — now I remember: SeaBIOS has a \"real mode\" code section that it copies into low memory at boot time (because it needs to run with interrupts... hmm, actually I feel like it was the other way around).\n\nWait, let me actually rethink this. In SeaBIOS:\n- The main SeaBIOS code runs from the ROM region (0xE0000+) in 32-bit protected mode.\n- The 16-bit code for real-mode calls is at... `src/16bit/` — linked into the image, but where?\n\nHmm, from the SeaBIOS Makefile: `obj/16bit/...` is compiled with `-m16 -mcmodel=small`? And in `linker.ld` it's placed at... I think the 16-bit code is linked at 0x10000! Yes! Now I remember: SeaBIOS's linker script places the 16-bit real-mode code (the \"code16\" section) at 0x10000 in the final image, but... wait, but the file at 0x10000 contains `eb ef 66 8b b1...` — and that window was found at guest 0xE9F0A.\n\nHmm, so the file's 0x10000 region is at guest 0xE9F0A — the file is at base 0xD9F0A?? That's odd (not aligned). Unless it's partial: maybe the file's 0x10000 region matches at 0xE9F0A by coincidence (a 40-byte match is unlikely to be a coincidence).\n\nSo base B = 0xE9F0A - 0x10000 = 0xD9F0A. Then the reset vector (la