e reset vector at the end of the file is `jmp 0x00F0:0xE05B`?? EA 5B E0 00 F0: I need to double-check the byte order: EA = jmp far; next 2 bytes = offset (little-endian): 5B E0 = 0xE05B; next 2 bytes = segment: 00 F0 = 0xF000! (I read it backwards before!). Segment = 0xF000, offset = 0xE05B → physical = 0xF0000 + 0xE05B = 0xFE05B.\n\nSo the reset vector jumps to 0xFE05B — within the image (if the image is at 0xE0000: 0xFE05B - 0xE0000 = 0x1E05B... but the file is only 0x20000 bytes; 0x1E05B is valid). Let me check what's at file offset 0x1E05B!\n\nSo: CPU reset → 0xFFFF0 (last 16 bytes of the 128K image at 0xE0000 = 0xE1FF0...\n\nHmm, wait, no: if the 128K image is at 0xE0000, the last 16 bytes are at 0xE1FF0-0xE20000, not 0xFFFF0. The 0xFFFF0 reset location is...\n\nUnless the image is placed at 0xF0000 and is 64K?? But the file is 128K!\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me check QEMU's source for how it places bios.bin. In pc.c (QEMU 8.2):\n```c\nstatic void pc_cpuset... \nvoid pc_cpus_init(MachineState *machine)\n...\n    bios_name = qemu_get_bios_name();\n    ...\n    bios_size = ...\n```\nActually, for i440fx: `rom_add_file(bios_name, -bios_size, ...)` where bios_size = 0x20000 (128K) → loaded at 0x100000 - 0x20000 = 0xE0000.\n\nAnd the reset vector: QEMU's pc.c also has:\n```c\n    /* Load the initial 31-byte reset code into ... */\n    cpu_memory_write_debug? \n```\nI recall: `pc_reset()` writes the reset code:\n```c\n    static const uint8_t reset_code_31[] = {\n        0xFB, 0x31, 0xCD, 0x10, 0xCD, 0x16, 0xCD, 0x17, 0xF8,\n        0x8E, 0xD0, 0x8E, 0xC0, 0x8E, 0xE0, 0xFC, 0x88, 0xE6,\n        0x30, 0xD8, 0xFC, 0x31, 0xCD, 0x10, 0xCD, 0x16, 0xCD, 0x17,\n        0xFB\n    };\n```\nHmm, not quite. Actually I recall it like this: QEMU copies the last 16 bytes of the BIOS to 0xFFFF0 if the BIOS is smaller than 64K? Or it writes its own 16-byte reset stub at 0xFFFF0: `ljmp 0xF000:0xE05B`?\n\nYes — that's it! From QEMU's pc.c (hw/i386/pc.c, `pc_reset` or `fw_cfg` initializatio