n the ROM (the 0xC0000-0x100000 region). And at runtime, SeaBIOS copies it to 0x10000 (the \"realmode\" copy)... with patching? Hmm.\n\nOK, let's go the practical route: I have the source. The stuck function is at some offset in the code16 section, and EIP=0x10D01 with the loop `jne 0x10D01`. In the 16-bit code, this pattern:\n\n```asm\n    ...\n.Lloop:\n    test something\n    jne .Lloop\n```\n— a wait-for-condition loop. In SRCBOTH, which functions contain a `test`/`jne` self-loop (a wait loop)? Classic candidates:\n- hw/ps2port.c: `ps2_wait`? Wait for the PS/2 keyboard's status register: read port 0x64, wait for the output buffer to be empty...\n- hw/ata.c: wait for the drive to be ready: `while (ata_inb(port|7) & 0x80)`...\n- hw/timer.c: pit wait?\n- hw/floppy.c: floppy wait loops (lots of them!)\n- hw/pci.c: pci conf wait?\n\nThe `jne .` self-loop: in C code, `while (inb(port) & bit);` compiles to: out/in, test al,al, jnz loop. That's exactly `test $x,%al; jne .`—a 2-3 instruction loop! And EIP=0x10D01 sits right on the `jne .` (the last instruction of the loop, self-jumping because the loop is 1-2 instructions... no wait, a `test+jnz` loop is 3 bytes: test is 2 bytes (a8 xx) + jnz is 2 bytes (75 xx) = 4 bytes, jnz at offset+2 jumps back by -2... jnz self (75 00) happens when the loop is exactly... hmm: `test al,al` (2 bytes: 84 c0? No, that's 2 bytes) + `jnz` (75 00 = 2 bytes): if jnz jumps to itself, then the loop body is... jnz 0x10D01 jumps to itself. For that, the preceding instruction is at 0x10D01-? Hmm, the byte at 0x10D00 is 0x5F (pop di)—that's not part of the loop.\n\nWait, let me re-verify: the loop is exactly just `jne 0x10D01` (1 instruction, 2 bytes: 75 00)?? That would be an unconditional infinite loop (since nothing in between changes the flags). GDB displayed `0x10d01: jne 0x10d01`—the bytes at 0x10D01-0x10D02 are 75 00.\n\nFor `jne .` to be meaningful, the loop must be exactly 2 bytes (jnz itself) and be entered from a preceding instruction that sets the flags—so the \"loop\" = [test/set-flags instruction at 0x10D00?]... but 0x10D00 is 0x5F (pop di). Hmm, that breaks the theory—unless the loop entry is from 0x10D00: pop di; then jne... no, pop di doesn't set flags.\n\nOr maybe the loop is larger and 0x10D01 just happens to be... no, EIP is fixed at 0x10D01 across many samples → each iteration is exactly 1 instruction at 0x10D01 → the instruction at 0x10D01 jumps to itself → an infinite spin regardless of flags (since the flags don't change, if ZF=0 then jne always... if ZF=1 it falls through to 0x10D03).\n\nSo: something jumped to 0x10D01, and 0x10D01 is `jne .` → infinite spin (ZF=0). This is a \"wait\" loop that can't terminate—the condition is never satisfied because... wait, no: if this loop is a `while(cond);` with a port read, the port read should be part of the loop. A bare `jne .` with no read in between = a bug, or the loop is being used as an intentional \"spin forever\" (like `halt()`/`panic()`-style spin).\n\nOr—this is a misaligned jump into the middle of code. The real flow: some `jmp`/`call` target got miscalculated (a relocation bug in this build?), landing in the middle of an instruction stream where a `75 00` happens to be located.\n\nLet me look at the actual SeaBIOS 16-bit layout. I'll check scripts/layoutrom.py to see where code16 gets placed, and find the offset of the stuck function within code16. The guest's code16 is at 0x10000. The file's code16 is at some ROM offset X (per the linker script generated at build time). The Ubuntu build should have generated the same layout. Let me find where in the 128K bios.bin file the code16 section is. From layoutrom.py's logic: code16 is placed in... let me read the script.\n\n\n\nLet me examine layoutrom.py to see where the 16-bit code sits in the ROM image.\n\n",
