      "thought": "I see, the 16-bit code goes into the f-segment (0xF0000-0x100000) of the ROM image. In a 128KB image (0xE0000-0x100000), the f-segment portion is the upper 64KB of the file: file offset 0x10000-0x20000 = physical 0xF0000-0x100000.\n\nSo in the 128KB bios.bin:\n- File 0x00000-0x0FFFF (64KB): the \"e-segment\" region (physical 0xE0000-0xF0000) — contains 32-bit code, data, etc.\n- File 0x10000-0x1FFFF (64KB): the f-segment (physical 0xF0000-0x100000) — 16-bit code + reset vector.\n\nAnd the code at GUEST 0x10000 (0x10000-0x10D10) — where does this come from? It's not in the file… so is it a runtime copy of the 16-bit code from the f-segment to low memory (0x10000)??\n\nThat's it! Now I clearly remember: SeaBIOS copies the 16-bit real-mode code to low memory (0x10000) at boot time. Why? Because in real mode the code needs to be below 1MB… no, the f-segment is below 1MB. Hmm. The real reason (from SeaBIOS's git history): the 16-bit code is copied to low memory so it can be relocated and used after the main BIOS… actually I remember the real reason: the code in the f-segment is where SeaBIOS lives, and when SeaBIOS hands off to the OS boot, it needs the 16-bit code (for int86 calls during boot, etc.) to be at a fixed low address…\n\nWhatever the reason, the flow is: `rom_reset()`/`copy16` copies code16 from 0xF0000+ to 0x10000, then the boot process runs 16-bit code from 0x10000.\n\nNow — the code at guest 0x10000 doesn't byte-match the file at 0x10000 (f-segment start). Let's verify: does guest 0x10000-0x10D10 match file 0x10000-0x10D10 with an offset applied? The earlier window test found no 40-byte match anywhere in the file. But relocations could differ… actually, the copy should be an exact byte copy (the linker has already placed it at the final address). Hmm, unless layoutrom's finalsegloc differs from the copy destination.\n\nWait, actually — the copy might come from a different section. Let's check: maybe the 16-bit code isn't being copied, and