      "thought": "Jackpot! Found the actual root directory at disk 0x48000. This is a genuine Windows 3.11 install:\n\n- Entry 0: `IO     SYS` attr=07 (archive+hidden+system) start=5 size=40774\n- Entry 1: `MSDOS   SYS` attr=07 start=15\n- Entry 2: `DOS` (directory) start=2\n- Entry 3: AGENT, 4: WINDOWS (directory, start=830), 5: TCP, ...\n- Entry 14: `COMMAND COM` attr=01 start=25\n- Entry 20: `AUTOEXEC BAT` start=13826\n- Entry 19: `CONFIG SYS` start=21319\n- Etc.\n\nSo the actual root directory is at disk 0x48000 = LBA 576. The IO.SYS file starts at cluster 5.\n\nNow, per the BPB layout (data starts at LBA 222, cluster size 8): cluster 5 → LBA 222 + (5-2)*8 = 246. IO.SYS (40774 bytes) starts at LBA 246.\n\nThe boot sector loads the first 3 sectors of IO.SYS = LBA 246, 247, 248 → disk offset 0x9780-0x9DFF.\n\nBut the guest's 0x7C00 = disk 0x63650+ (the string table region)! Not disk 0x9780!\n\nSo the boot sector loaded the wrong data — it loaded from 0x63650 (LBA 795+0x50) instead of 0x9780 (LBA 246).\n\nHmm, wait. Unless the FAT maps cluster 5 to a different LBA! The cluster→LBA mapping depends on where the data region actually starts, and that depends on the true geometry of the partition, not the (possibly lying) BPB. If the actual data region starts at LBA 671 (per my earlier calculation of reserved=512): cluster 5 → LBA 671 + 3*8 = 695. That's not 795 either.\n\nIf data starts at LBA 793: cluster 5 → 793+24 = 817. No.\n\nLet me solve it: for cluster 5 to map to LBA 795 (where the string block starts... no wait, the loaded block started at LBA 795+0x50, which is a sub-sector position — a sector loader can't do that).\n\nHmm, hmm, hmm. Wait — maybe the block loaded to 0x7C00 doesn't start at the file's first cluster! Let me think again about the boot sector code:\n\nMSDOS5.0 boot sector:\n1. `b9 0b 00` = 11 entries; `be e6 7d` = SI=0x7DE6?? Hmm, in the dump: `0d0: b0 01 e8 ac 00 72 16 8b fb b9 0b 00 be e6 7d f3 a6` — hmm, `be e6 7d` = mov si, 0x7DE6. And `f3 a6` = rep movsb. This copies... from DS:SI (0x7DE6) to ES:DI (0x7C00+?)...\n\nHmm, actually let me re-read the DOS 5.0 boot sector code:\n```\n00c0: bb 00 05 8b 16 52 7c a1 50 7c e8 92 00 72 1d\n        mov bx, 0x0500\n        mov dx, [0x7C52]\n        mov ax, [0x7C50]\n        call 0x...  (read sectors: INT 13 AH=2, count in CX)\n        jc 0x...\n00d0: b0 01 e8 ac 00 72 16\n        mov al, 1\n        call ...     (verify boot disk?)\n        jc 0x...\n00d8: 8b fb            mov di, bx        (DI = 0x0500)\n00da: b9 0b 00         mov cx, 11        (11 entries)\n00dd: be e6 7d         mov si, 0x7DE6\n00e0: f3 a6            rep movsb         (copy 11 bytes?? no — 11 iterations of 1 byte: copies 11 bytes from 0x7DE6 to 0x0500)\n00e2: 75 0a            jne 0x...\n00e4: 8d 7f 20         lea di, [0x207F]\n00e7: b9 0b 00         mov cx, 11\n00ea: f3 a6            rep movsb         (copy 11 bytes from 0x207F to 0x2000+... hmm)\n00ec: 74 18            jz 0x...\n00ee: be 9e 7d         mov si, 0x7D9E\n00f1: e8 5f 00         call 0x...\n00f4: 33 c0            xor ax, ax\n00f6: cd 16            int 0x16         (keyboard!)\n00f8: 5e               pop si\n00f9: 1f               pop ds\n00fa: 8f 04            pop es\n00fc: 8f 44 02         pop ds\n00ff: cd 19            int 0x19         (boot from drive)\n0102: 58 58 58         pop ax; pop ax; pop ax\n0105: eb e8            jmp 0x...\n```\n\nOK, this is getting really deep. The standard DOS 5.0 boot sector logic:\n1. Load 11 root directory entries (352 bytes) from the root directory to 0x7D00.\n2. Scan entries: first look for a volume label; if entry 0 is a volume label, skip it; look for the first entry that's not a directory with the hidden+system attributes (0x03)? Actually, in DOS 5.0: \"if entry[0] is the volume label, use entry[1]; otherwise use entry[0]\" — something like that. And check (attr & 0x08) == 0 (not a directory).\n3. Take the start cluster of that entry and read 3 sectors from the data region to 0x7C00.\n\nReading