apacity) differs from what the boot sector assumes, then the LBA→CHS conversion produces the wrong CHS, and QEMU converts CHS→LBA using its own geometry → wrong LBA!\n\nThis is a classic problem: the DOS boot sector does the LBA→CHS conversion itself (using the BPB's geometry of 16 sectors, 63 heads — wait, actually no! The MSDOS5.0 boot sector uses LBA (INT 13 AH=8) if the BIOS supports it... or does it use CHS via AH=2?\n\nLet me recall the DOS 5.0 boot sector code: it uses INT 13 AH=2 (CHS) — with C,H,S computed from LBA using the BPB geometry (sectors/track=16, heads=63)? Hmm, actually I think DOS 5.0's boot sector uses LBA (AH=8) when available...\n\nNo, actually, not. Looking at the disassembly above:\n```\n0110: f7 e3 03 06 49 7c 13 16 4b 7c bb 00 07 b9 03 00\n        mul [0x7C49]     (EAX × sectors/track? hmm)\n        sub [0x7C4B], 3\n        mov bx, 0x0700\n        mov cx, 3\n0120: 50 52 51 e8 3a 00 72 d8\n        push ax; push dx; push cx\n        call 0x15A  (INT 13 routine)\n        jc ...\n```\nThe routine at 0x15A:\n```\n0150: 70 00 ac 0a c0 74 29 b4 0e bb 07 00 cd 10 eb f2\n        ...\n015a: 3b 16 18 7c 73 19 f7 36 18 7c fe c2 88 16 4f 7c\n        ...\n```\nHmm, and there's also a CHS computation routine:\n```\n0160: 3b 16 18 7c  cmp di, [0x7C18]\n        ...\n0180: c3 b4 02 8b 16 4d 7c b1 06 d2 e6 0a 36 4f 7c 8b ca 86 e9 8a 16 24 7c 8a 36 25 7c cd 13 c3\n        ret\n        mov ah, 2\n        mov dx, [0x7C4D]\n        mov cl, 6\n        shl cl, 6?? ...\n        mov cx, [0x7C4F]\n        mov ch, [0x7C24]\n        mov cl, [0x7C25]\n        int 0x13\n```\n`b1 06 d2 e6 0a 36 4f 7c`: mov cl, 6; shl cl, 6; or cl, [0x7C4F]... this is a CHS computation: CL = (LBA % 16) | (head << 6), CH = (LBA / 16 / 63) & 0xFF, DH = head & 0xFF...\n\nSo YES — the DOS 5.0 boot sector uses CHS (INT 13 AH=2) and converts LBA→CHS using the BPB geometry (sectors/track = 16, heads = 63).\n\nNow the problem: QEMU presents the disk with some geometry. QEMU computes geometry from