eads = 2;\n    if (cyls > 0x400) {\n        sectors = 16;\n        heads = 16;\n        cyls = capacity / (heads * sectors);\n    }\n    ```\n    cyls=32: 32 < 512 (0x200) and 32 > 0x10 (16)? YES (32 > 16) → heads = 4!\n    Then check cyls > 0x400: no.\n    So QEMU geometry: heads=4, sectors=63, cyls=32. Total = 32×4×63 = 8064 sectors?? Way less than 523089!\n\n    Hmm, that can't be right — QEMU shouldn't report a geometry that can't address the whole disk. Let me recall QEMU's actual code (hw/i386/pc.c? no — in `qemu/...` the geometry heuristic is in `include/hw/ide.h` / `ide_get_conf`? The standard function is `get_drive_geometry` in block.c?\n\n    Actually, the well-known QEMU geometry heuristic (from block.c, `bdrv_get_geometry`? or the \"standard BIOS geometry\" in `util/...`):\n    ```c\n    void drive_get_geometry(BlockBackend *backend, uint64_t *nb_sectors,\n                            int *cyls, int *heads, int *sectors)\n    ```\n    Hmm. There's a well-known algorithm (\"standard CHS\" by Ralf Brown's INT 13H):\n    ```\n    # of cylinders = (capacity / 63 / 255) + 1?\n    ```\n    Ralf Brown's standard:\n    - if capacity < 16384: 255 heads? ...\n    The \"standard\" algorithm (Ralf Brown):\n    ```\n    heads=255, sectors=63\n    cyls = (capacity + heads*sectors - 1) / (heads*sectors)\n    if (cyls < 0x1000 && cyls > 0x200) heads=16\n    if (cyls < 0x100 && cyls > 0x10) heads=4\n    if (cyls <= 0x10) heads=2\n    if (cyls > 0x400) { sectors=16; heads=16; recompute cyls }\n    ```\n    For 523089: cyls = ceil(523089/16050) = 33 (since 32×16050=513600 < 523089). 33 < 512, 33 > 16 → heads=4. cyls = 33 (unchanged, heads only changed... wait, does the algorithm recompute cyls after changing heads? In Ralf Brown's algorithm, cyls is computed once from 255/63, and then heads is adjusted without recomputing cyls (that's the nature of this heuristic — it produces an \"optimized\" geometry where cyls×heads×sectors >= capacity may not hold!).\n\n    Result: cyls=33, heads=4, sectors=63 → 33×4×63 = 8316 sectors < 523089. This geometry cannot address the whole disk — the standard heuristic is designed for \"display\" geometry, and LBA (AH=28) is used for the actual addressing.\n\n    So INT 13 AH=15 (get parameters) would report C=32 (0-based), H=3, S=63.\n\n    Now, the DOS 5.0 boot sector does LBA→CHS using the BPB geometry (16/63): LBA 246 → (cyl=0, head=15, sect=6). Then it calls INT 13 AH=2 with that CHS. QEMU converts CHS→LBA using QEMU's own geometry (4 heads, 63 sectors): LBA = (0×4 + 15)×63 + 6 = 15×63+6 = 951. (Using 1-based: ((c×h)+h)×s + s: ((0×4)+15)×63 + 7-1... = 945+6=951.)\n\n    LBA 951 (partition-relative) → absolute LBA 63+951 = 1014 → disk offset 1014×512 = 0xFEE80. Hmm, that's not 0x63650 either.\n\n    Hmm, close but not quite. What if QEMU reports heads=16 (not 4)? LBA = (0×16+15)×63+6 = 16×63-63+6 = 945+6 = 951. Same (cyl 0). If QEMU geometry is 16/63: (0,15,6) → LBA 951. Same.\n\n    For the result to be LBA 795-63=732 (partition-relative): CHS (0,15,6) → LBA 732 = 15×H + 6 → H = (732-6)/15 = 48.4. Not an integer.\n\n    Hmm. So CHS (0,15,6) with any standard geometry doesn't produce 732.\n\n    Wait — maybe the LBA the boot sector computed isn't 246. Let me recompute: the boot sector reads the start cluster of the IO.SYS directory entry = 5 (from the root directory at 0x48000, entry 0: start=5). But wait — the boot sector reads the root directory from the LBA it computed (based on the BPB: LBA 190, which is garbage), not from 0x48000!!!\n\n    The boot sector's root directory LBA = reserved + nfats×spfat = 1 + 126 = 127 (partition-relative) → absolute LBA 63+127 = 190. That's the garbage region (the free-space pattern) I saw earlier!!!\n\n    So the boot sector read 11 \"directory entries\" from LBA 190 (the garbage pattern) and found a \"boot file\" with a garbage start cluster!\n\n    From the LBA 190 dump earlier: entry 0: name \"Ø $< u\" attr=0x1F start=12031; entry 1: \"WèDÿ¸ÿ À¿\" attr=0x10 start=12031; entry 2: \"þË\" attr=0x00 start=14 LBA=695 (in my old calculation); etc.\n\n    DOS 5.0 boot sector logic (from the code at 0x160):\n    ```\n    0160: 3b 16 18 7c  cmp di, [0x7C18]    ; compare with 0x7C18 (11×32+2? hmm: 0x7C18 - 0x7D00 = -0x1E8... hmm)\n    ```\n    Hmm, let me not go down this rabbit hole. The point: the boot sector uses the (garbage) entry at LBA 190, gets a garbage start cluster (say, 12031 or 14), computes LBA = 159 + (X-2)×8, does the LBA→CHS conversion (16/63 geometry), reads 3 sectors via INT 13 AH=2, QEMU converts CHS→LBA using QEMU's geometry, and the resulting LBA is... something that lands at disk 0x63650 (absolute LBA 795, partition-relative 732) — with a 0x50 offset (hmm, the sub-sector offset still doesn't make sense...).\n\n    Hmm, wait, actually, let me double-check the 0x50 \"offset\". The guest's 0x7C00 matched disk 0x63650 for the window at +0x000. But what if the loaded sector actually started at 0x63600 (LBA 795, sector-aligned) and only the first window search found the best match at +0x50 because the first 0x50 bytes were modified by the stage 1 code (which self-patched the text \"Please insert...\" region)?\n\n    The on-disk LBA 795 starts with: \"nsert disk with %1 in drive %2\\r\\n!Press any key to continue . . .\\r\\n\\x1c\\r\\nTerminate b\" + \"atch job (Y/N)?\"...\n\n    The guest's 0x7C00 starts with \"atch job (Y/N)?\" — the first 0x50 bytes on disk (\"nsert disk...Terminate b\") are missing from the guest. If the code had loaded from 0x63600, the guest would start with \"nsert disk...\". The guest starts directly with \"atch job\". So the guest really is missing those 0x50 bytes → the load really did start at 0x63650 (or the first 0x50 bytes were overwritten with... something else? The guest's 0x7C00+0x00 = \"atch job (Y/N)?\" — for that to be an overwrite, the code would have had to write \"atch job (Y/N)?\" exactly there — which is the content of 0x63650! So it's more likely a load from 0x63650.)\n\n    A sub-sector load start = the loader is not sector-based, or... the LBA→CHS→LBA conversion produced an LBA, and the CHS→LBA conversion at QEMU's geometry produces an arbitrary LBA (not necessarily the intended one), and 732 just happened to be the result. But the 0x50 sub-sector offset within a 512-byte sector read?? INT 13 AH=2 reads 512-byte sectors — you can't start at +0x50 within a sector!\n\n    Unless the \"3 sectors\" were read to 0x7C00 one at a time, and... no, even then it's sector-aligned.\n\n    OK, you know what — I've spent a huge amount of time on forensics. Let me step back and think about what matters:\n\n    The goal: get the VM to the Windows 3.11 desktop. The boot is broken because the disk image's geometry assumptions don't match QEMU 8.2's behavior. The task says \"this image is known to work with QEMU 5.2.0\" — strongly suggesting I should use QEMU 5.2.0!\n\n    Why would QEMU 5.2.0 boot correctly while 8.2 doesn't? Geometry! QEMU 5.2 and 8.2 may report different geometry for this disk, or handle CHS→LBA differently. Or — the image's boot code was specifically crafted to work with QEMU 5.2.0's specific CHS behavior (e.g., QEMU 5.2's geometry for a 523089-sector disk is 16/63 or something that matches the BPB, making the CHS round-trip lossless).\n\n    Let me think: if QEMU reports geometry = 16 sectors/track, 63 heads (matching the BPB), then the LBA→CHS (16/63) → LBA (16/63) round-trip is lossless, and the boot works correctly (reads the right LBA, and the root directory at 0x48000 is... wait, no — the boot sector reads the root directory from LBA 190 per the BPB, which is still garbage...\n\n    Hmm, wait, is that right? Let me re-verify: is LBA 190 really the root directory per the BPB? reserved=1, 2 FATs × 63 sectors: FAT1 is LBA 64-126, FAT2 is 127-189, root directory is 190-221. Yes, LBA 190 per the BPB. And the actual root directory is at 0x48000 = LBA 576.\n\n    LBA 576 = partition-relative 513. Hmm: 513 = reserved' + nfats'×spfat'. If reserved'=512-1=511... let me try: if the real BPB had reserved=512 (0x0200): FAT1 = LBA 64-126 (63), FAT2 = 127-189, root directory = 190. No, that's not it.\n\n    For the root directory to be at LBA 576 (partition-relative 513): 513 = reserved + nfats×63. If nfats=1: reserved = 450. If nfats=2: reserved = 387. Hmm, neither is a \"clean\" number.\n\n    What if spfat is different? If spfat=126 (two 63-sector FATs... no.\n\n    Hmm, if the actual geometry were: reserved=1, nfats=1, spfat=63 → root directory = 1+63 = 64 → LBA 127. No.\n\n    Let me look at this from the FAT's perspective: find where the FAT table actually is! The FAT is a table of 16-bit values. Let me search for the start of the FAT on disk: it should start with the media byte (0xF8 for a hard disk... or 0xF0/0xFF) + 0xFFFF (free) + the first cluster (0x0002 or 0x0000/0xFFFF). Let me search for the pattern: f8 ff ff ff 02 00 0f 00 (IO.SYS starts at cluster 5, MSDOS.SYS starts at 15 (0x0F), the DOS directory is at 2...).\n\n    The actual FAT (if it matches the real root directory): entry 2 = DOS directory's start=2; entry 5 = IO.SYS (40774 bytes, cluster size 8×512=4096 → 10 clusters: 5..14); entry 15 = MSDOS.SYS (38138 bytes → 10 clusters: 15..24); entry 25 = COMMAND.COM (54645 → 14 clusters: 25..38)...\n\n    FAT: [0]=F8xx, [1]=FFFF, [2]=F8xx (end of directory chain), [3]=FFFF, [4]=FFFF, [5]=06, [6]=07, [7]=08, [8]=09, [9]=0A, [0A]=0B, [0B]=0C, [0C]=0D, [0D]=F8xx, [0E-0F]=FFFF FFFF, [10]=11, ..., [17]=F8xx, [18-19]=FFFF FFFF, [1A]=1B...\n\n    Let me search for the byte pattern: F8 FF FF FF 02 00 F8 xx FF FF FF FF 06 00.\n\n\n\nThe real root dir (at 0x48000) reveals a genuine Win 3.11 install with IO.SYS at cluster 5. Now let me find the real FAT table and pin down the true partition layout.\n\n",
