      "thought": "QEMU 8.2's `info block` no longer displays the CHS geometry (it was removed in the newer QEMU). Let's get the geometry via QMP: `query-block`... this doesn't include CHS either. The INT 13 AH=15 geometry is computed inside QEMU's ide/ahci code.\n\nActually — the important thing is to figure out which CHS→LBA conversion QEMU 8.2 performs for an IDE drive with capacity 523089 sectors, and what it was in 5.2.\n\nIn QEMU, for IDE, the CHS translation is done in `blk_get_geometry` / the standard heuristic. Let's look at QEMU 8.2's source (we have the seabios source. We can similarly grab the qemu 8.2 source — or reason from known behavior).\n\nActually — better idea: test empirically in the running VM! The guest is stopped in code that makes INT 13 calls. We can use the GDB stub to read the guest's state... but an easier way: write a small test that uses the VM's current INT 13 state... hmm, complicated.\n\nAlternative: QEMU's standard geometry heuristic (from hw/block/... in QEMU, in QEMU 8.x it's in `block/io.c`? or `include/block/block.h`... the function is `bdrv_get_geometry` → calls `blk_get_geometry` → for a raw file it uses `blk_aio_get_geometry` → ultimately `bdrv_get_device_geometry`... and the CHS heuristic is in `block.c`??\n\nI remember the QEMU standard heuristic (from `util/...`, no — `hw/i386/pc.c`? no). It's the same algorithm Ralf Brown documented (the \"standard\" geometry):\n\n```c\n/* From Ralf Brown's INT 13h list */\nstatic void drive_get_geometry(uint64_t capacity, int *cyls, int *heads, int *sectors)\n{\n    int cyls, heads, sectors;\n    /* This is the \"standard\" heuristic used by the BIOS */\n    sectors = 63;\n    heads = 255;\n    cyls = (capacity + heads * sectors - 1) / (heads * sectors);\n    if (cyls < 0x1000 && cyls > 0x200) {\n        heads = 16;\n    }\n    if (cyls < 0x100 && cyls > 0x10) {\n        heads = 4;\n    }\n    if (cyls <= 0x10) {\n        heads = 2;\n    }\n    if (cyls > 0x400) {\n        sectors = 16;\