contiguous 1536 bytes from disk 0x63650, and then the code modified parts of it. OK.\n\nSo the CHS→LBA result must have been absolute LBA 795 (partition-relative 732).\n\nWhat CHS (with QEMU's geometry) maps to 732? If QEMU's geometry is (H=4, S=63): 732 = (C*4+H)*63 + S. 732/63 = 11.6 → H_track = 11 (0-based track index within the cylinder... let me parameterize: LBA = (C*H + h)*S + s. 732 = (C*4+h)*63+s. (C*4+h) = 11 (732 = 11*63 + 39 → 11*63=693, 732-693=39 → s=39, C*4+h=11). If C=0: h=11, s=39 → CHS (0, 11, 39[1-based 40]).\nIf QEMU's geometry is (H=16, S=63): 732 = (C*16+h)*63+s: 732 = 11*63+39 → C*16+h = 11 → C=0, h=11, s=39 → CHS (0,11,39).\nIf (H=255, S=63): C*255+h = 11 → C=0, h=11, s=39.\n\nSo the CHS the boot sector must have passed was (C=0, H=11, S=40[1-based]).\n\nThe boot sector computes CHS from LBA using the BPB geometry (S/T=16, H=63):\nLBA → CHS: s = LBA % 16 (0-based sector = LBA%16, 1-based = LBA%16+1); t = LBA/16; h = t%63; c = t/63.\n\nFor the result to be (c=0, h=11, s=39[0-based 38]): s=38 → LBA%16 = 38?? Impossible (LBA%16 < 16)!!!\n\nSo it's impossible with 16 sectors/track. Therefore the boot sector must have used a different sectors/track in its CHS conversion!\n\nIf the boot sector's BPB said sectors/track = 63: s = LBA%63 = 38 → LBA = k*63+38; t = LBA/63 = k; h = k%63 = 11 → k = 11 (c=0); LBA = 11*63+38 = 732!!! ✓✓✓\n\nSo the boot sector converted LBA 732 using sectors/track=63, heads=63 (or any value ≥12) → CHS (0, 11, 39[1-based]) → QEMU (with any of the H∈{4,16,255} and S=63 geometries) → LBA 732.\n\nSo the LBA the boot sector computed for the file start was 732 (partition-relative), and the geometry it used for the CHS conversion had sectors/track=63.\n\nNow: 732 = reserved' + nfats'*spfat' + rootsectors' + (cluster-2)*spc, using the values the boot sector took from the (possibly modified) BPB...\n\nAnd the \"cluster\" came from a directory entry read from LBA 190 (garbage data) — or from wherever the boot sector read the root dire