      "thought": "Now the screen is visible! Let's try decoding it:\n\nLines 160-176: A window with a gray (+) background in the center of the screen (roughly x=130-570).\nLines 194-206: White text inside the window:\n```\n##...##   ##...##     ######...########.####   #####...####  #####..#####\n####.##   ####.##     ##..##..#..##..#..##     ##...##..##   ##....##..##\n##.####..##...##      #####......##.....##     #####...##.## ##..######..\n##...##..##...##      ##..##.....##.....##     ##...##..##   ##..##......\n##...##..##...##      ##..##.....##.....## ..##..##...##...## ##...##....##\n```\nThis is the classic \"Starting Microsoft Windows 3.1\" style text... Let me try reading the characters:\nRow 1: \"##...## ##...##\" = \"M M\"? Actually `##...##` = M, `##...##` = M, then a space, `######` `########`...\n\nLet me try to read more carefully — it's a 2x-scaled font (each character is about 8px wide / 8px tall, displayed at 720x400).\n\nLine 194: `##...##..##...##....######...########.####......#####.....####....#####....#####`\nDecoding 8x8 glyphs (each 7px + 1 spacing? let me try grouping):\n- `##...##` → M\n- `##...##` → M\n- `..`\n- `######` → ?\n- `########`?\n\nHmm, let me try a different approach — the text on lines 258-270 (lower part of the screen, right of center):\nLine 262: `#####....###.##..##...##..##...##..##...##...##..##....##..##..##...##....##..##...##..##...##...##...##..##...##..##..##...##...##...##...##...##`\nThis might be \"Please wait...\" or a path string.\n\nHmm, honestly, decoding 8x8 bitmap text in ASCII is tough. Let me do it properly: this looks like VGA 80x25 text mode (720x400 is a 72x40 character grid? 720/8=90, 400/8=50 — hmm, QEMU's 720x400 = 90x50 cells at 8px each... unusual; standard 80x25 is 640x400. 720x400 is what QEMU uses for MDA/CGA? No... well, whatever).\n\nLet me extract the glyph at each cell and compare against the CP437 font... QEMU's VGABIOS contains the CP437 font. An easier approach: use the font bitmap from the SeaBIOS vgabios (vgabios-stdvga.bin contains the font) to decode the text.\n\nAn even simpler approach: just render it at higher fidelity (no 2x vertical compression) and squint at it. Let me try outputting with each character cell as a single character using a proper 8x8 font table extracted from vgabios-stdvga.bin (the font is at a known offset — usually at 0x2800 in the VGA BIOS, 256 glyphs × 64 bytes = 16KB).\n\nLet me extract the font from /usr/share/qemu/vgabios-stdvga.bin (or the one in the seabios directory — the same /usr/share/qemu). Font offset in vgabios: search for the characteristic pattern of glyph 0 (space = all zeros) and glyph 'A'. Even simpler: the VGABIOS font starts right after a long run of zeros, followed by the 'A' glyph: bytes 00 08 02 02 02 02 3E 02. Let me try searching for this pattern.\n\n\n\nLet me decode this screen properly using the CP437 font from the VGA BIOS.\n\n",
