      "thought": "Okay, let's decode these. The font is 8x14, but only the top ~10 rows are meaningful (the glyph content is mostly in rows 0-9. My 10-row dump covers the main body, and the remaining rows 10-13 are descenders or blank).\n\nHmm, wait — the dump shows 10 rows (y0..y0+9). The glyphs are:\n\n**Body line 1** (white text, x=45..419):\n- c00: `##...##. / ###..##. / ####.##. / #######. / ##.####. / ##..###. / ##...##. / ##...` — M! (M with a diagonal)\n  Actually: rows: `##...##.`, `###..##.`, `####.##.`, `#######.`, `##.####.`, `##..###.`, then `##...##.`... hmm, that's 8 rows. Then rows 7-9? I only dumped 10 rows. Row 7 is `##...##.`, and the remaining rows are probably also `##...##`. Yes, it's \"M\".\n- c01: all blank up to row 3, then `..#####. / .##...## / .##...## / .##...## / .##` — that's the top of \"S\"? Rows: row 3 `..#####.`, rows 4-7 `.##...##`... this is an \"S\" starting from row 3 (the glyph is 8x14, and the cap height starts at row 3? No wait...).\n\nHmm, I'm confused. Let me rethink: in the 8x14 font, the uppercase glyph height is 12 rows (rows 2..13?) and the x-height is 8 rows. No, actually, in the 8x14 font: the uppercase is 11px tall starting from row 1, and the lowercase is 8px starting from row 5.\n\nc00's \"M\": `##...##. / ###..##. / ####.##. / #######. / ##.####. / ##..###. / ##...##. / ##...##. / ##...##. / ##...##.` — the M's diagonal reaches the bottom at row 3, and after that it's a vertical stroke. In the 8x14 font, the M spans rows 0..13: the diagonal goes down to the center (around row 6), and after that it's a vertical stroke. My dump only shows 10 rows, so I'm seeing the top part. Well, whatever, it's clearly \"M\".\n\n- c01: blank rows 0-2, then `..#####. / .##...## / .##...## / .##...## / .##` — hmm, rows 3-7. If the uppercase is from row 1... this pattern looks like the top part of \"S\": `..#####. / .##...## / ##....#. / ....##.. / ....##.. / ...##... / .##..##. / ..###...`? But c01's row 3 is `..#####.`, rows 4-7 are `.##...##`... hmm, that doesn't match S (S's row 1 should be `..#####.`).\n\nWait — maybe the font isn't 8x14 but a different height, and the cell division is off. The white line is y=194..206 = 13 rows. Let me dump y=192..208 (16 rows) per cell to see the full glyph.\n\nNo wait, actually, let me reconsider. Maybe these aren't 8px cells starting from x=45. The x range of the white is 45..419 = 375px = 46.9 cells. The text is \"Microsoft Windows 3.1\"? M-i-c-r-o-s-o-f-t (9) + space + W-i-n-d-o-w-s (7) + space + 3.1 (3) = 21 characters. That's only 168px. Not 375.\n\nHmm. Let me count the non-blank cells in body line 1: c00 (M), c01 (something), c03, c04, c05, c06, c07, c08, c09, c10, then blank c11-12, c13, c14, c15, c16, c17, c18, c19, c20, c21, c22, c23, c24, c25, c26, c27, c28, c30, c31, c32, c33, c34, c35, c36, c37, c39, c40, c41, c42, c43, c44, c45.\n\nThis is a long line of about 40+ characters. Something like \"Microsoft Windows 3.1 for Workgroups\"? That's 33 characters. Or maybe \"Starting Microsoft Windows 3.1 for Workgroups\" = 42 characters! That matches (about 42 cells, with c02, c11, c12, c29, c38 being spaces).\n\nLet me decode: \"Starting Microsoft Windows 3.1 for Workgroups\"\n- c00 = M? But the first character should be \"S\"...\n\nHmm, wait. Unless the line is \"Microsoft Windows 3.1 for Workgroups\" (33 characters)... but there are 40+ non-blank cells.\n\nLet me carefully decode each cell using the 8x14 font. Let me get the full 14 rows per cell. Let me dump 14 rows.\n\nActually, let me rethink. Let me look at the title bar (gray text) first. That might be the title of the dialog:\n\n**Title bar** (c00..c41, but c00, c12, c13, c17, c25, c27, c28... are filled with `########` — those are probably the left border or the window's edge intruding into my cell grid. The interior of the title starts from x=189, but the border is at 148-156, so... hmm, c00's x=189 shows `########` in all rows — that's the border, not text. Wait, in the earlier y=165 scan the gray started at 189... and in the first rendering (x=100) I saw the `#########` border at x=148-156. So there are two gray regions at y=165: the left border (148-156), then the interior (navy), and then... no wait, at y=165 the x range of gray is 189..530, and the border is 148..156. So at y=165: gray at 148-156 (border), gap at 157-188 (navy interior), and gray at 189-530 (text glyphs!). But in my dump, c00 (x=189) = `########` in all 10 rows — that contradicts the navy gap...\n\nOh wait. I get it — in the title bar, the glyphs are drawn in the same gray color as the border, and c00 being `########` in all rows = a vertical bar = maybe it's part of the first character, or... hmm, c00 is 8px of gray in all rows. Then c01: `#..###.. / #...##.. / #....#.. / #....... / #..#.... / #..##... / #..###..` — that's \"W\" (W = left vertical + diagonal + right vertical: rows: `#......#`? no). Let me think: W in 8x8: `10000001 / 10000001 / 10000001 / 10000001 / 10000001 / 01000100 / 00101000 / 10000001`. Hmm, c01 shows `#..###..` in row 0 — a `#` in the left column.\n\nHonestly, the title bar text is: let me look at the pattern c01-c11 and c14-c16, c18-c21, c22-c24, c26, c29-c41.\n\nAlternatively — maybe the title bar text isn't centered text but a full-width \"Welcome to Windows 3.1\" with the Windows logo?\n\nLet me step back and think about what this screen is.\n\nActually — wait. Let me rethink the whole thing. Gray band 16px + a white line below... maybe this is the Windows 3.1 \"Welcome\" screen: the actual Win3.1 Welcome dialog has a title bar \"Welcome to Windows 3.1\", a large Windows logo (4 panes), and text \"Click a mouse button to continue.\" Hmm, but the logo panes should be colored... in monochrome they'd be shades of gray.\n\nHmm, but where's the logo? The gray band might be the top part of a large dialog, and the rest of the dialog is... invisible (navy on navy)? The dialog should have a white/gray border. The bottom border is at y=172-175. So the window is only 16px tall.\n\nUnless the window border is only drawn where it's gray, and the rest is... no, that's not right.\n\nOkay, another thought — maybe this is a Windows 3.1 SETUP screen. The image's boot program (the custom INT 21h one we found in the forensics, the \"atch job (Y/N)?\" prompt) — that's the disk's custom boot program. But the current screen is the result of that boot program having executed something.\n\nLet me look at the whole screen again: navy background, a thin gray horizontal band (16px, 545px wide) at y=160, and two white text lines below it (y=194-206, y=258-270). The text lines are at x=45..419 and x=243..465 — roughly centered (720/2=360; line 1 center ≈232, line 2 center ≈354).\n\nHmm, the title bar band is at x=148..693, center=420.\n\nLet me decode properly. Let me dump the full 14 rows per cell for the body text, and also reconsider: maybe the font is 8x8 and each \"character\" is 16px wide (double-width)? 375px / 16 = 23 characters. \"Microsoft Windows 3.1\" = 21 characters + spaces = 23!\n\nLet me test that hypothesis: group in 16px cells.\n\n\n\nLet me re-examine with the possibility of double-width (16px) characters and full glyph height.\n\n",
