      "thought": "Only LBA 0 (MBR) and LBA 63 onwards (boot sector + IO.SYS etc.) are non-zero. There's no hidden custom boot program in LBA 1-62!\n\nSo the boot flow is: MBR (standard) → LBA 63 (MSDOS5.0 boot sector) → IO.SYS (LBA 64 onwards) → COMMAND.COM → AUTOEXEC.BAT → WIN.COM → the Windows 3.1 startup sequence.\n\nAnd the whole thing takes about 4 seconds?! On QEMU with a fast CPU (this machine is a 9950X!), a 32MB, 386-class VM… actually, is it possible? QEMU emulates i486 (the default CPU for \"pc\" is qemu32/i486?). On a 5GHz 9950X, QEMU 5.2's TCG can execute several hundred million instructions per second… the Win 3.1 startup on real hardware (16MHz 386) takes 30-60 seconds. The speedup from QEMU TCG on a 9950X could be 10-50x… so a 4-second startup is possible!!\n\nSo this really is Windows 3.1 booting in CGA mode! The cyan screen = a CGA-mode Windows 3.1 startup screen (a \"Starting Windows 3.1\" splash with a window frame drawn in CGA cyan on a black background — hmm, actually, in CGA 4-color mode, background=blue, foreground=white/cyan/magenta…).\n\nWait, the cyan screen has a black background (0,0,0) with cyan text. CGA 4-color mode: background is either black or blue. In the first frame the background was black, and in the later dialog frames the background is navy (0,0,168). So the display mode was switched from a CGA black-background mode to a blue-background mode.\n\nAnd the cyan screen's window: a 3D raised frame (top-left = light cyan (56,151,183)??, bottom-right = dark cyan (56,167,199))… CGA's bright colors = the same as dark. Whatever.\n\nSo the sequence is:\n1. t≈2-3s: A CGA screen with a large window + 2 lines of text (cyan on black).\n2. t≈4s onwards: A navy screen with a title bar + 2 lines of white text + a blinking cursor (blue background).\n\nBoth are part of the Windows 3.1 CGA startup sequence! And the navy dialog waits for input (a blinking cursor after line 2).\n\nWhat is the navy dialog? In the CGA-mode Win 3.1 startup: after \"Starting Windows 3.1\" comes the \"Welcome to Windows 3.1\" dialog… and in CGA mode, the welcome dialog…\n\nHmm, wait — actually, I remember the CGA welcome screen! It's a dialog with a title bar, and inside it: a Windows logo made of CGA colored blocks (cyan/magenta/white) and the text \"Click a mouse button to continue\". The 16px navy band + 2 lines of white text + cursor is…\n\nHmm, no. Let me look at the layout of the navy screen again:\n- Title bar band: x=188-693, y=160-175 (16px tall)\n- Line 1 (white): x=45-419, y=194-206\n- Line 2 (white): x=243-465, y=258-270\n- Cursor: x=477-485, y=269-270\n\nInteresting: line 1 starts at x=45, line 2 starts at x=243. The title bar is at x=188. These don't align with any single window — the text lines extend left and right beyond the title bar (x=45 < 188). So the \"title bar band\" is not the title bar of a window containing those text lines.\n\nSo maybe: the navy screen has a separate small window (the 16px band at x188-693 — a 16px-tall window is a \"toolbar\"/\"menu bar\"?), plus 2 lines of independent white text on the navy background.\n\nHmm, hmm. In CGA-mode Win 3.1, the desktop background is… navy (blue). The \"Welcome\" dialog should be gray. The 16px band is gray — could it be the title bar of a gray dialog whose body is below it? But the bottom border of the band is at y=172-175 (4px)… a window should continue below it. Unless the rest of the window is also navy = invisible?? No, a window's border should be visible.\n\nOK, yet another idea: maybe this is the \"Program Manager\" desktop! In CGA mode, the Program Manager desktop = a blue background with a title bar at the top (\"Program Manager\") and menu bar (File Options Window Help) + a window list of group icons. But the band is in the middle of the screen (y=160), not at the top (y=0)…\n\nHmm, wait, is it though? Screen height is 400. y=160 is the middle. No.\n\nOK — time to stop guessing and interact intelligently. What we know:\n- The navy dialog is waiting for a key (blinking cursor at the end of line 2).\n- When I pressed Enter, it went to a black screen (dead state).\n\nWait — was that black screen really \"dead\"? It stayed black for over 60 seconds. Hmm, but maybe the black screen is a transition state: after Enter, the program starts the next phase (the actual Windows loading) with a black screen… and takes a few minutes? On a 9950X, the next phase should also be fast (a few seconds), but.\n\nHmm, actually, wait: let me reconsider the black screen. After Enter: pure black (0,0,0). The pre-reset cyan screen also had a black background. So the black screen might be the same CGA black-background mode with nothing drawn (or waiting). Maybe Enter caused the program to exit to a \"blank\" state (crash), or maybe it started loading in the background.\n\nLet me try again: reset, wait for the navy dialog, then instead of Enter, try other keys:\n1. First, try a mouse click (in case the welcome dialog is asking for a mouse click).\n2. If no change: try a character (Y, N, S, O…).\n3. Observe the result each time.\n\nBut actually — hmm, before that, let me try to correctly decode the two text lines on the navy dialog one more time. Because the content determines the response. Let me do a serious template-matching decode:\n\n1. Collect the glyph shapes of all the navy text (I have them: line 1 has 42 slots, line 2 has about 25 slots, plus the title bar).\n2. Build a template library from the characters I'm confident about (M, o, T, L, E, O, S, 5, R, W, a, n, t, e, i, u, c, r, G, A, U, K, P, F, B, D, H, I, J, …).\n3. For each unknown glyph, find the closest template (Hamming distance).\n\nThe problem is: I only have a few confirmed characters. But the same screen has repetitions: for example, the title's word 2 = line 1's word 2 (identical). And within the text, the same character appears in multiple slots.\n\nLet me collect all the distinct glyphs and cluster them, and label the clusters using my confident decodings:\nConfid