      "thought": "Big discoveries:\n\n1. **0x1bbcab1 region = PROTOCOL.INI contents!** Looking at it:\n   - `...FileSharing=Yes...LogonDisconnected=yes...UserName=JJ...Workgroup=WORKGROUP...ComputerName=QEMU...Comment=Jaap & Jo...logonvalidated=no`\n   - `[network drivers] netcard=PCIND.DOS transport=ndishlp.sys devdir=C:\\WINDOWS LoadRMDrivers=No`\n   - `[MSTCP] EnableRouting=0 Interfaces=RTL80290 deadgwdetect=1 pmtudiscovery=1`\n   - `[DNS] DNSServers=192.168.0.1 HostName=qemu DomainName=lan`\n   - `[Svga256.drv] Resolution=1 svgamode=95 Chipset=CirrusLogic 5436/46 LatchCapable=Yes`\n   - `[RTL80290] DefaultGateway=192.168.0.1 IPMask=0.0.0.0 IPAddress=0.0.0.0 Description=RealTek 8029 (REALPCI) Binding=RTL8029`\n   - `[NBT] LANABASE=0 EnableProxy=0 EnableDNS=0`\n   - `[CL_WinA...`\n\n   So PROTOCOL.INI has MSTCP interfaces=RTL80290, and the RTL80290 driver is bound to... The driver is \"PCIND\" (a PCI IDE? No — \"PCIND\" = PCI NDIS driver? \"ndis2=8:PCIND.DOS ndis3=7:PCIND.386 mlid=PCIODI.com\" — from the OEMSETUP.INF at 0x5b5d062: the driver files are PCIND.DOS, PCIND.386, PCIODI.com — this is a generic PCI NIC driver (PCIND = \"PCI NDIS\"?) that supports RTL8029.\n\n   So Windows 3.1 is configured with the Workgroup networking (NETCFG): PCIND driver + NDIS + MSTCP, bound to the RTL80290 interface.\n\n   At Windows startup, WININET/NW16 loads the PCIND.386 driver → probes for a PCI RTL8029 card → not found → warning \"No RTL8029 adapter exists on your system!\" is displayed (probably by the PCIND.386 VxD or a helper) → \"Press any key to continue\".\n\n2. **0x333b0c6 region** — another driver's message set (\"Loading PCI Ethernet Network Adapter\", \"No Network Adapter Found!\", \"More than one Network adapters have been detected!\", \"ID comparing...Warnning:--- Can not find [AdapterID] in PROTOCOL.INI...\") — this is the message table of the PCIND driver.\n\n3. **[Svga256.drv] Chipset=CirrusLogic 5436/46 svgamode=95** — the display driver is the SVGA 256-color driver configured for the Cirrus Logic 5436/46 chip! Interesting — but the screen is CGA... meaning the SVGA driver probably failed to detect the Cirrus chip (QEMU's std VGA is not a Cirrus) and fell back to CGA mode! That explains the CGA palette!\n\nSo the actual boot flow:\n- DOS 5.0 boots (IO.SYS + CONFIG.SYS + AUTOEXEC.BAT)\n- WIN.COM starts Windows 3.1 (CGA mode because the SVGA driver couldn't find the Cirrus chipset → falls back to CGA 16-color)\n- Networking (Workgroup) initialization: loads PCIND.386 (NDIS RTL8029 driver)\n- PCIND.386 probes the PCI bus for an RTL8029 PCI card → not found (QEMU's pc machine has no PCI RTL8029; ne2k_isa is ISA, and rtl8139 has a different vendor/device ID)\n- Displays a warning: \"No RTL8029 adapter exists on your system!\" / \"Press any key to continue\" (CGA mode dialog)\n- User presses a key → ... black screen for 60 seconds → dialog again??\n\nHmm, wait — why does the dialog come back after the key press? Let me think about the PCIND driver's logic (from its message table):\n- \"Loading PCI Ethernet Network Adapter\"\n- \"No Network Adapter Found!\"\n- \"More than one Network adapters have been detected!\"\n- \"ID comparing... Warnning:--- Can not find [AdapterID] in PROTOCOL.INI or can not find one that match... Driver can not determine which one to use... Solution:--- Please specify and check adapter ID in PROTOCOL.INI file... [Example]: [XXXXXXX] AdapterID = @00123456789A\"\n- \"More than one Fast Ethernet PCI adapters have been detected! SL...\"\n\nHmm, and the LE file at 0x424e000 contains \"No RTL8029 adapter exists on your system!\" + \"No RTL8029 Adapter Warnning Message\" + \"Registering adapter # buffers %ld, I/O base addr 0x%lx, interrupt number %ld, max multicast %ld\" + \"Novell 2000 Adapter. BoardNum 0x%x\" + \"PCIND\".\n\nWait, \"Novell 2000 Adapter\" + \"BoardNum 0x%x\" — this is a Novell (NetWare) related string... and \"PCIND\"...\n\nOK, here's the deal: the LE file at 0x424e000 is probably \"PCIODI.COM\" or the \"PCIND\" DOS-side ODI driver (the \"mlid=PCIODI.com\" in the INF — the ODI driver for DOS!). Hmm, but it has an LE header (a Windows program)...\n\nNo, actually, wait: maybe it's the \"Realtek 8029 OEMSETUP\" — the [netcard] section of the OEMSETUP.INF at 0x5b5d062: `RTL8029=\"RealTek 8029 (REALPCI)\",000,ndis,ethernet,0x07,rtl8029,rtl8029_nif` — this is NETCFG's OEMSETUP.INF.\n\nAnyway — the important question is: what makes the dialog loop? And what's the correct way to proceed?\n\nLet me look at the LE file more carefully. This is a 32-bit Windows LE program. Let me properly parse the LE header (the e_lfanew=0x80 one) and disassemble the entry point. The code is probably doing:\n1. Probe PCI/ISA for the RTL8029\n2. If not found: switch video mode to CGA, display dialog, wait for key\n3. Then... return an error / exit\n\nAnd the caller (the NDIS framework / NWLINK) might retry, or the \"black screen for 60 seconds\" might be a retry loop with a delay.\n\nLet me properly disassemble this LE file. Parse the LE header:\n\nLE header (from 0x80):\n```\n0x80: 4c 45           'LE'\n0x82: 00 00           magic = 0x0000  (should be 0x0184!)\n0x84: 00 00           alignment\n0x86: 02 00           reserved\n0x88: 04 00           checksum (low)\n...\n```\nHmm wait, let me re-read the actual bytes from the dump:\n\n`4c45 0000 0000 0000 0200 0400 0000 0000 0080 0200 0900 0000 0300 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0010 0000 0016 0000 0094 0400 0000 0000 007f 0000 0000 0000 00c4 0000 0003 0000 000c 0100 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000 0030 0100 0039 0100 0000 0000 0000 0000 0043 0100 006b 0100 00d7 0500 0000 0000 00d7 0500 0000 0000 0008 0000 0006 0000 0016 8800 0041 0000 ...`\n\nStandard LE header layout:\n- +0x00: sig 'LE'\n- +0x02: magic (0x0184)\n- +0x04: alignment\n- +0x06: reserved\n- +0x08: checksum (4)\n- +0x0C: flags (4)\n- +0x10: physical address of first data page (2)\n- +0x12: number of header pages (2)\n- +0x14: offset of relocation table (4)\n- +0x18