s RTL8029 and others — \"RealTek 8029 (REALPCI)\"). It probes the PCI bus for specific vendor/device IDs (Realtek 0x10ec:0x8029). QEMU's rtl8139 is 0x10ec:0x8139 — a different device ID, so it won't match an RTL8029 probe.\n\nHmm!! So a standard QEMU NIC won't satisfy the RTL8029 probe (unless the driver falls back to probing ISA I/O ports — the \"I/O base addr\" string suggests I/O port based init: the \"Registering adapter # buffers %ld, I/O base addr 0x%lx, interrupt number %ld, max multicast %ld\" — this is the RTL8029 ISA driver's registration message).\n\nHmm, wait — but the INF says `ndis2=8:PCIND.DOS ndis3=7:PCIND.386 mlid=*:PCIODI.com` with drivername=PCIND$, param=AdapterID. \"PCIND\" = PCI NDIS. So it's a PCI driver. It probes the PCI bus.\n\nCan I make QEMU 5.2 present a PCI device with vendor 0x10ec device 0x8029? No — QEMU has no RTL8029 device.\n\nAlternative: what if the probe falls back to ISA I/O ports (0x300-0x3E0) if PCI isn't found? The presence of \"I/O base addr 0x%lx\" in the registration string suggests I/O port configuration is involved. The classic RTL8029 probe (for the ISA version) scans I/O base 0x300, 0x320, ...: writes to base+2, reads back, or writes an ID.\n\nSo if the driver first probes PCI (fails on QEMU), then falls back to ISA I/O port probing (0x300-0x3E0), then a QEMU ne2k_isa at 0x300 might be found by the fallback (if the fallback uses a generic NE2000-style probe that the QEMU NE2000 can pass).\n\nI need to disassemble the probe code to know for sure. Let me do it right now. Load /tmp/rtl8029com.bin in gdb, disassemble around the references. But first I need to find the code section. Let me dump 0x100-0x600 to look for the code, and also look for where the string \"No RTL8029 adapter exists\" (0x5e90) is referenced (look for lea instructions pointing there).\n\nActually — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider the whole thing again. I've been assuming the dialog is drawn by this LE file. But the dialog is drawn in CGA 8x14 text mode