      "thought": "Now I can see the PCI probe code clearly:\n\n```\n55a3: mov $0x10ec, %edi          ; vendor = Realtek\n55a8: mov 0x1c(%esp), %eax       ; bus number (loop counter 0..7)\n55ac: or $0x40, %ax              ; bit 6 set (enable)\n55b0: add %ax, %ax               ; ×2 → shift 4: bus×2 << 4... \n55b3: push %eax                  ; \n55b4: push $0xcf8                ; \n55b9: push $0\n55bb: call 0x1180                ; outl(port 0xcf8, value) — write to PCI config address register\n...\n55c3: mov $0xc000, %ebx          ; config data base 0xc000 (start of config space mapping)\n55c8: mov $0x10, %esi            ; 16 devices per bus\n...loop over 16 functions:\n55d0: lea 0x1a(%esp), %eax\n55d4: push %eax                  ; &out (vendor)\n55d5: push %ebx                  ; 0xc000 + device offset?\n55d6: push $0\n55d8: call 0x121b                ; inl from 0xc000+off → reads vendor:device\n55e0: cmp %di, 0x1a(%esp)        ; vendor == 0x10ec?\n55e5: jne 0x56b3                 ; if not, next\n55eb: ... read 0xc000+2 → device ID\n55fe: cmpw $0x8029, 0x18(%esp)   ; device == 0x8029?\n5605: jne 0x56b3                 ; if not, next\n; Found RTL8029!\n560b: read 0xc000+0x10 → BAR0 (I/O base)\n561b: andw $0xfffe              ; clear the I/O bit\n5622: read 0xc000+0x3c → (0x3C is the... hmm, 0x3C is the vendor ID in the config space header! Wait)\n```\n\nHmm, wait, let me re-read. The offsets being read: 0xc000 (vendor+device), 0xc000+2 (device), 0xc000+0x10 (BAR0), 0xc000+0x3c (?), 0xc000+0x80 (push $0x80 → read 0xc0080 = ...).\n\nWait: `56b3: push $0x80; push $0xcf8; call 0x1180` — this writes 0x80 to 0xcf8?? Hmm, that's weird. Actually looking at it: 0x1180 is `out` (push value, push port, call). At 56b3: push 0x80 (value?), push 0xcf8 (port)... then 56c7: add $0x100, %ebx (advance by 0x100 per device — 16 functions × 0x100 per function = 0x1000 per bus), dec esi (16 functions), loop.\n\nAnd the outer loop: 56d4: incl 0x1c(%esp) (bus counter), cmp $0x8 (8 buses 0..7), loop back to 55a8.\n\nSo: this code scans PCI buses 0-7, 16 devices each, and for each device: reads config word 0 (vendor) from the mapped config space at 0xc000+offset, and if vendor == 0x10ec (Realtek) and device == 0x8029 (RTL8029) → found. It reads BAR0 (0xc000+0x10) & 0xFFFE = I/O base, and reads 0xc000+0x3C...\n\nWait, 0x3C within the 256-byte PCI header = offset 0x3C = vendor ID again?? No: the PCI config header is 256 bytes: 0x00 vendor, 0x02 device, 0x04 command, 0x06 status, ..., 0x10-0x1F BAR0-5, 0x34 IRQ line, 0x3C-0x3F ... hmm, offset 0x3C in a type 0 header is... actually in the standard type 0 header: 0x34 = IRQ line, 0x35 min latency, 0x36 revision, 0x37 header type, 0x38-0x3B = data pointer (type 1 only), 0x3C = ... (class code is 0x08-0x0B). Offset 0x3C in type 0 is usually unused/zero.\n\nHmm, wait — `562a: push lea 0x3c(%ebx)` reads 0xc000+0x3C with `andw $0xff` (16-bit) — hmm.\n\nAnd 0xc000+0x80 (at 56b3... no wait, that's a write to 0xcf8 with value 0x80...\n\nHmm, actually the calls at 56b3/56b8: `push $0x80; push $0xcf8; call 0x1180` — 0x1180 is `out` (outl port, value? or out value, port?). Let me check the signature of 0x1180: from 55b3-55bb: `push %eax; push $0xcf8; push $0; call 0x1180` — 3 arguments: (eax, 0xcf8, 0). If 0x1180 is `void outl(port, value, ctx)`, then arg order is (value?...) — C calling convention: first pushed = last argument. Pushed in the order: %eax, then 0xcf8, then 0. So args = [0, 0xcf8, eax] → f(ctx=0, port=0xcf8, value=eax) → outl(0xcf8, eax). Yes: 0x1180 = OUTL(port, value).\n\nAnd 0x121b = INL(port, &value): from 55d0-55d8: push &out, push 0xc000, push 0 → f(ctx=0, port=0xc000, &out) → inl(0xc000, &out). Yes.\n\nSo at 56b3: outl(0xcf8, 0x80)?? That would set the PCI config address register to 0x80 — hmm, wait, that's in the middle of the \"next device\" path (56b3 is the \"not found\" branch target, and also... hmm, 56b3: push 0x80; push cf8; call outl → outl(0xcf8, 0x80)?? That doesn't make sense as a PCI config operation...\n\nOh wait. Maybe 0x1180 isn't outl — maybe it's a \"PCI write\" helper: (ctx, address_register_port=0xcf8, value). Hmm, but that's the same as outl.\n\nHmm, outl(0xcf8, 0x80): writes 0x80 to the PCI config address register = disable/zero the config space mapping (bus 0, device 0, function 0, offset 0x80 → actually 0x80 = offset 0x80 in the config space, bus 0 device 0 function 0, with the enable bit clear (bit 31 is 0) → access to config space is disabled). This is a cleanup before moving on to the next device — disable the config mapping. OK, that makes sense!\n\nSo the probe is: **a pure PCI config space scan for vendor 0x10ec, device 0x8029, across 8 buses × 16 devices.**\n\nIf found: read BAR0 (0xc000+0x10), mask with 0xFFFE → I/O base; read 0xc000+0x3C... hmm wait, `5622: lea 0x22(%esp),%eax; add $0xc,%esp (pop); push %eax (&out); lea 0x3c(%ebx),%eax; push %eax; push $0; call 0x121b` → inl(0xc000+0x3C, &out16) and `andw $0xff` — read a 16-bit value at config offset 0x3C, mask to 8 bits...\n\nHmm, offset 0x3C in a type 0 PCI header... hmm, whatever it is (maybe a Realtek-specific register or the \"AdapterID\" — the string \"ADAPTERID\" in PROTOCOL.INI!). Recall the help text: \"Input adapter's ID... [Example]: AdapterID = @00123456789A\" — a 12-digit hex ID. The driver reads a hardware \"adapter ID\" from config offset 0x3C?? Hmm — Realtek RTL8029 PCI cards have a unique \"board ID\"... Actually, for the purpose of distinguishing multiple RTL8029 cards. Whatever.\n\nThen it stores the I/O base in an array at 0x5824 (up to N adapters, count at 0x52a4, flag at 0x52a8).\n\n**Key conclusion: the driver only finds the adapter via a PCI config space scan for 10ec:8029.** QEMU's rtl8139 is 10ec:8139 → doesn't match. ne2k_isa → not PCI → doesn't match. e1000 → 8086 → doesn't match.\n\n**None of QEMU 5.2's built-in NICs will be found by this driver.**\n\nSo how on earth can this VM reach the desktop?!\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-read the flow. After the scan: if found → \"Registering adapter...\" → continues. If not found → warning dialog \"No RTL8029 adapter exists on your system!\" → \"Press any key to continue\" → after key press → ???\n\nLet me find the code for the \"after key press\" path. After the dialog + key press, what does the driver do? Options:\n(a) Return an error → the NWLINK/NDIS stack continues without networking → Windows boots to the desktop (with a networking error, but the desktop!).\n(b) Retry the scan in a loop → the observed black screen loop.\n\nThe observed behavior (black screen for 60 seconds, then the same dialog reappears) suggests (b)... or the caller is retrying the driver load.\n\nHmm, wait — actually, let me reconsider the \"black screen for 60 seconds\": what if it's not the driver retrying, but Windows is retrying the whole network initialization (the NWLINK VxD init fails → NWLINK retries after a delay)? The 60-second delay could be a fixed retry interval.\n\nIf the retry is infinite (the driver never succeeds because there's no 10ec:8029 card), then the VM will never reach the desktop with this QEMU configuration.\n\nBut the task says the image is \"known to be compatible with QEMU 5.2.0\" and asks us to \"leave the VM running at the Windows 3.1 desktop\". So there must be a way!\n\nIdeas:\n1. **Add a PCI device with vendor 0x10ec, device 0x8029.** QEMU 5.2 doesn't have an RTL8029 PCI device... but can I add a custom PCI device? Not without building QEMU with a custom device. I do have the QEMU 5.2.0 source at /tmp/qemu-5.2.0! I could add a minimal RTL8029 PCI device (config space only!) to QEMU and rebuild!\n\n   Actually — the driver only reads config space (BAR0 → I/O base) + probably reads a few I/O ports for the \"adapter ID\" (0x3C in config? no wait, that's config space). Let me check what I/O port operations happen after detection... The \"Registering adapter # buffers %ld, I/O base addr 0x%lx, interrupt number %ld, max multicast %ld\" — then it initializes the NIC at the I/O base. If my fake PCI device provides: config space with 10ec:8029, BAR0 = 0x300 (I/O space), plus working I/O ports at 0x300 (NE2000-like or RTL8029-like register behavior), the driver might initialize successfully!\n\n   QEMU 5.2's rtl8139 device code already exists — I can copy it and make a minimal \"rtl8029\" PCI device with vendor 0x10ec, device 0x8029, reusing the ne2000/rtl8139 backend. Then rebuild QEMU (I've built before; an incremental rebuild is fast — just add one file and re-link).\n\n2. **Check whether the driver's I/O port init can be satisfied by a QEMU ne2k_isa at 0x300** — but the PCI scan has to succeed first, and that requires the 10ec:8029 config entry.\n\n3. **Modify the overlay** (not the base image!): the guest can modify its own disk (snapshot). But what modification would help? PROTOCOL.INI — remove the [RTL80290] interface / netcard=PCIND entry → the driver isn't loaded at all → no warning → boot to the desktop! But the disk is \"immutable\" in the task (snapshot mode, base image unchanged). Modifying files in the overlay = modifying the guest's disk (permissible under -snapshot? The intent is: the base image is preserved; the guest can write freely). Hmm — but is that \"cheating\"? The task says \"QEMU in snapshot mode (base image immutable)\" — snapshot mode means writes don't persist to the base. Modifying the overlay via QEMU is... actually not directly possible — the overlay is managed by QEMU; you can't easily edit the overlay while the VM is running.\n\n   But — the guest itself could modify PROTOCOL.INI (via a command prompt)... except there's no DOS prompt — the boot goes straight into the Windows driver init.\n\n4. **Intercept the boot via QEMU monitor**: no clean way.\n\n5. **Wait and see**: maybe after N retries, the driver gives up and continues. The retry loop might have a limit. I've only observed one cycle (60 seconds). Maybe after 3-5 cycles it gives up. Worth observing for a while (5-10 minutes).\n\nLet me first try