      "thought": "The LE header is corrupted (magic=0, the group table is garbage data — my parsing read the wrong offset; the \"group header\" at 0x1000 is code, not a group header). This \"LE file\" has a fabricated/corrupted LE header, and only the MZ part and the code/data are real.\n\nI'm going way too deep into reverse engineering this driver. Let me step back and think about a practical solution.\n\n**Practical plan:**\n\nThe driver scans the PCI bus looking for 10ec:8029. QEMU 5.2.0 doesn't have that device. I have the QEMU 5.2.0 source at /tmp/qemu-5.2.0. I can add a minimal RTL8029 PCI device to QEMU:\n- vendor 0x10ec, device 0x8029\n- BAR0 = I/O space at 0x300 (size 0x100... the driver reads BAR0 & 0xFFFE)\n- IRQ\n- I/O port region that behaves like a Realtek RTL8029 (or at least returns plausible values so the driver's init succeeds)\n\nThe driver's post-discovery init does the following (from the registration message): \"Registering adapter # buffers %ld, I/O base addr 0x%lx, interrupt number %ld, max multicast %ld\" — it sets up buffers at some memory location, writes to the I/O registers (NE2000-compatible: page start addresses, command register, transceiver, etc.).\n\nIf I implement the I/O ports as an NE2000 (QEMU already has the ne2000.c backend! `net/slirp` no — `hw/net/ne2000.c` exists in QEMU 5.2), I can reuse it: create a PCI device with 10ec:8029 that internally uses NE2000's I/O port emulation (ne2000 registers: base+0 = command, base+1 = transceiver, base+2..5 = DSA, base+6..13 = ISR/RSR/RSR, base+14.. = TSR, base+16+ = data).\n\nThe RTL8029 is NE2000-compatible (that's the whole premise of the driver — the \"PCIND\" generic driver supports NE2000-family chips). So the NE2000 register behavior should satisfy the driver's init (it'll write to the command register, read the transceiver type, set up the page start addresses, etc.).\n\n**Plan:**\n1. Copy QEMU 5.2.0 source (already configured/built at /tmp/qemu-5.2.0).\n2. Add `hw/net/rtl8029.c`: a PCI device (vendor 0x10ec, device 0x8029, class ethernet) with a 0x20-byte I/O BAR, wired to a NE2000 backend (or a simple register file).\n3. Rebuild qemu-system-i386 (incremental build — fast).\n4. Boot QEMU with `-netdev user,id=n1 -net nic,netdev=n1,model=rtl8029` (or a custom device name).\n5. The driver finds the card → \"Registering adapter...\" → no warning dialog → boot continues → desktop!\n\nRisks:\n- The driver might do RTL8029-specific probes that NE2000 behavior fails (e.g., writing a specific pattern to a specific register and expecting an RTL8029-specific response). But the RTL8029 is a superset of NE2000, and the driver is designed for the RTL8029; the NE2000-compatible subset should be fine.\n- The driver might check the \"adapter ID\" (a 12-digit hex from config offset 0x3C? no, that was a 16-bit read with 0xff mask at offset 0x3C... hmm wait, let me re-check: `562a: lea 0x3c(%ebx)` reads config offset 0x3C. For a real RTL8029 PCI card, offset 0x3C is... in the standard type 0 header, 0x3C is reserved (0). Hmm, but then `andw $0xff` → value 0 → the \"adapter ID\" = 0? And it compares that against the AdapterID from PROTOCOL.INI ([RTL80290] doesn't have AdapterID set → default?). The code at 563f: `cmpb $0x1, 0x52a8` (a flag = \"multiple adapters found?\") — if multiple, compare the ID against a list at 0x52a4... otherwise store it. If the ID read is 0 and PROTOCOL.INI doesn't specify one, it's fine.\n\nActually, wait — looking at the code again:\n```\n5622: lea 0x22(%esp), %eax     ; &out2 (for 0x3C read? no...)\n5626: add $0xc, %esp\n5629: push %eax                 ; &out\n562a: lea 0x3c(%ebx), %eax      ; 0xc000+0x3C\n562d: push %eax\n562e: push $0\n5630: call 0x121b               ; inl(0xc000+0x3C, &out)\n5635: andw $0xff, 0x22(%esp)    ; mask the low byte\n```\nRead config offset 0x3C (in a type 0 PCI header... hmm, 0x3C is actually where the \"vendor ID\" would be in a header at 0x0C... no. Let me not overthink this: 0x3C in a 256-byte config space — for type 0, bytes 0x34-0x3F are: 0x34 IRQ line, 0x35 min latency, 0x36 revision, 0x37 header type, 0x38-0x3B reserved (data pointer for type 1), 0x3C-0x3F = ... hmm, actually in the standard header layout, 0x3C is inside the \"capability pointer\" area? No — the capability pointer is at 0x34 for type 0? No: type 0: 0x34 = interrupt line, 0x35 = min grant, 0x36 = revision, 0x37 = header type, 0x38-0x3B = data pointer (type 1 only), 0x3C = ...\n\nHmm, whatever it is — a real RTL8029 card returns some value there. QEMU's PCI config space: for unimplemented offsets, it returns 0. The driver masks with 0xFF and... uses it as the \"adapter ID\" for disambiguation. If it's 0 and PROTOCOL.INI doesn't specify AdapterID (it doesn't — [RTL80290] has no AdapterID), the driver accepts it.\n\nHmm wait, but actually — the PROTOCOL.INI section for this NIC: `[RTL80290] DefaultGateway=... IPMask=... IPAddress=... Description=RealTek 8029 (REALPCI) Binding=RTL8029`. No IOBASE/INTERRUPT parameters. So the driver uses BAR0's I/O base (from PCI config) — good, my device provides BAR0=0x300.\n\nSo let me also think about what happens after \"Registering adapter\": the driver (as part of NWLINK/NDIS init) sets up the NIC: writes to the command register (base+0), sets the transceiver (base+1, e.g., 0x22 for BNC, 0x12 for AUI, 0x32 for MII), reads the transceiver type back... For the NE2000: write to the transceiver register base+1: QEMU's ne2000 accepts 0x00, 0x12 (AUI), 0x22 (BNC), 0x32 (MII) — returns it on read. RTL8029 also accepts 0x32 (MII). No problem.\n\nThen it sets up the page start addresses (base+2..5) — NE2000 accepts any 16-bit values. No problem.\n\nThen it enables interrupts, sets the multicast buffer, etc. All standard NE2000 operations — QEMU's ne2000 handles them all.\n\n**The main risk is IRQ handling**: the driver sets the interrupt line (from PCI config 0x34) and enables the NIC's interrupts. QEMU's ne2000 raises IRQ on receive (if a packet is sent via slirp). That's fine — the driver's ISR handles it.\n\nOK — but wait. Before building a custom QEMU (which is a big step), let me reconsider:\n\n**Alternative: check if the driver has a fallback to ISA probing.** Let me look at the other \"in\" instruction clusters (0x289-0x50c, 0xa48-0xa81, 0x20cc, 0x2698, 0x2d5e, 0x2dcf, 0x4580, 0x7294-0x7312, 0x5815, 0x6a09, 0x6c96, 0x716d). If there's an ISA probe (scanning 0x300-0x3E0), I could use a plain ne2k_isa without any QEMU modification!\n\nLet me disassemble around 0xa40-0xa90 and 0x20c0-0x2100 and check which ports they access.\n\n\n\nI found the header is corrupted, so I'll check the I/O port clusters to see if there's an ISA fallback probe before deciding to modify QEMU.\n\n",
