      "thought": "These regions (0xa20-0xb40, 0x2080-0x2150) are memory buffer management code (calls to 0x13f6 = memcpy, 0x1180/0x119c/0x11fd = port in/out helpers), not a separate ISA probe. Port I/O goes through the helpers 0x1180 (outl), 0x119c, 0x11fd — port number comes from `0xff(%esi)+offset` (the base I/O address stored in the device struct at offset 0xff).\n\nSo the whole driver is: PCI scan (10ec:8029) → get I/O base from BAR0 → operate on NE2000-style registers at that base.\n\n**Decision: build a QEMU 5.2.0 with a minimal RTL8029 PCI device (NE2000 register set at I/O base 0x300).**\n\nLet me check the state of the QEMU 5.2.0 build tree and add the device.\n\nNo wait, hold on. Before going down this path, let me consider one more alternative that might be much simpler:\n\n**What if I don't need the driver to find the card — what if the \"Press any key\" loop eventually terminates?**\n\nObserved: Enter → 60s of black → same dialog again. If this cycle repeats forever, no good. If it terminates after N cycles... I should test: keep pressing Enter, count the cycles, and observe over 5–10 minutes.\n\nLet me do this right now (it's cheap — just automate Enter + monitoring). If it terminates, I don't need to build QEMU. If it doesn't terminate, build QEMU with the RTL8029 device.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider the 60s of black once more. What is the guest doing during those 60 seconds? 10% CPU. 60s of black, then the dialog. If the driver re-scans PCI after the key press... the PCI scan is 8 buses × 16 devices = 128 config reads — that's milliseconds, not 60 seconds. So the 60s of black is a deliberate delay (a timer wait) — a retry loop with a ~60s (1 minute) delay. Classic driver retry: \"retry in 60 seconds.\"\n\nIs the retry count limited? Many NDIS driver inits retry a few times and then give up. Or the NWLINK stack retries forever.\n\nLet me test with automation: press Enter, then automatically press Enter every time the dialog reappears, log each cycle (with timestamps), continue for about 8 minutes. If the cycles stop and the screen changes to something else (a desktop!), win. If it's the same dialog 5+ times, conclude it's an infinite loop → go with the custom QEMU NIC.\n\nLet me write that automation now.\n\n\n\nI've confirmed the driver is PCI-only with no ISA fallback, so I'll test the retry behavior by auto-pressing Enter and monitoring cycles to see if it gives up.\n\n",
