      "thought": "system_reset keeps failing in a flaky manner. This QMP flakiness is really getting to be a pain — the pattern seems to be: right after the previous QMP client disconnects, the first command times out, but retrying later works.\n\nNo, wait — let me look at this from a different angle. What's the difference between when it works and when it fails? When I tested manually (the 3-attempt test), it worked on the first try. The failing cases are all `system_reset` — and system_reset was working in the long test just now (0.0 second response).\n\nHmm, wait a sec… is there some connection-related state lingering inside QEMU that only clears when the guest does something?\n\nLet me look at this from QEMU's side: `server,nowait` chardev. In QEMU, when using the monitor with `server,nowait`, the chardev backend (chr_unix) does the following:\n- On connect (CHR_EVENT_OPENED): accepted.\n- On disconnect: closed.\n\nFor a unix server socket, QEMU 5.2's `qemu_chr_open_unix` with server=1: listen(); on connection, call chr_accept… with `nowait` the main loop handles it via the poll G_SOURCE.\n\nAh — I just remembered something important about QEMU's unix socket chardev: **when a client connects and then disconnects quickly, and a new client connects, everything is fine. But when the old client is killed, QEMU's poll watches the listening socket; a pending connection in the accept queue wakes up the main loop → accept → new client is connected. All normal.**\n\nSo why does the first command time out?! The greeting arrives (meaning accept + greeting send are working). qmp_capabilities gets a response (sometimes). Then the next command times out…\n\nHmm, hold on, let me re-examine the failing trace: line 11 = `q.cmd('system_reset')` inside my inline script, but before that, QMP.__init__ runs `self.cmd('qmp_capabilities')` — if that had failed, the traceback would show a different line. So __init__ succeeded (greeting + caps response OK), and then system_reset timed out 3 times (3×20 seconds = 60+ seconds).\n\nSo within a single connection: greeting OK, caps OK, then system_reset → no response for 60+ seconds.\n\nWhereas in the manual test: greeting OK, caps OK, query-status OK — all fast.\n\nDifference: system_reset vs query-status. **system_reset resets the machine.** What if the machine reset itself is taking an extremely long time because the guest is in a state where the reset can't complete?? In QEMU, `system_reset` → qemu_system_reset_request → main loop executes `qemu_system_reset()` — this is usually fast (ms). But it has to wait for all VCPUs to stop… and in TCG, resetting during VCPU execution… `qemu_system_reset` calls `cpus_reset()` — in TCG this is done by signaling each vcpu thread to reset. A vcpu thread spinning in a guest loop (R state, 10% CPU) — the reset request is checked in the execution loop… it should be quick.\n\nHmm, but what if the guest code is executing a long sequence with no interrupts… TCG vcpus process the interrupt request as soon as one is pending (KVM does; TCG: the tb generation loop checks `cpu_exec` for pending interrupts every TB). So the reset should take effect within a few TBs (microseconds to milliseconds of guest time).\n\nBut the vCPU is at 10% CPU, doing a loop with a delay (timer wait) — the timer interrupt fires, the guest handles it, loops. The reset request is a pending CPU interrupt — it should be picked up on the next TB.\n\nSo the reset should complete quickly. But it doesn't (60+ seconds). Something else is going on.\n\nOh, wait. I think I finally get it!!! **The QMP command response requires the main loop, and the main loop is blocked waiting for the BQL — and the BQL is held by the VCPU thread, which is waiting for something in a device model that never completes** — for example, a disk write to the qcow2 overlay… no, that's not it.\n\nHmm, or — **the vcpu thread is stuck in a `KVM`-like ioctl… no, it's TCG.\n\nLet me actually measure: during the QMP timeout, is the main thread (12468) in poll (idle) or blocked on a futex (BQL)? If it's in poll → the main loop is running, just not processing QMP (why?). If it's in futex wait → BQL contention.\n\nLet me do this: start a background loop that samples /proc/12468/task/*/wchan every 0.2 seconds for 70 seconds, and simultaneously send a system_reset from another process and observe.\n\n\n\nThe QMP flakiness correlates with `system_reset`. Let me instrument what QEMU threads do during a reset hang:\n\n",
