      "source": "agent",
      "thought": "Interesting! This time system_reset worked immediately (0.00 seconds). And the thread state is normal. The instability is real but non-deterministic.\n\nWait — I have a new hypothesis! Look at the pattern of when it fails: the failures happened right after I ran a long QMP session (the observation loop that holds the connection for 60-150 seconds). When bash's `timeout` kills the script, the socket gets closed... and the next connection fails on the first command.\n\nBut this time's test: the previous script (the one that timed out with system_reset) held the connection for 60 seconds, got killed, and then... the threadwatch+reset test connected and it worked.\n\nHmm, so what about the case where the previous client died with data still in the send buffer?\n\nActually — you know what, let me try a completely different theory: **the guest is sending data on the serial port or doing something that generates QMP events**... no, that's not it.\n\nNew idea: let me check whether the QMP instability correlates with the guest's 60-second probe cycle. The guest probes every ~60 seconds (the black-screen cycle). During the probe, the guest hammers PCI config ports 0xCF8/0xCFC (128 reads) and I/O ports. Each port I/O from the guest to QEMU requires the BQL. If the guest is doing a large burst of port I/O (say, 10k+ ops), the VCPU thread holds/releases the BQL rapidly, but the main loop should be able to slip in between...\n\nActually, TCG port I/O: the VCPU holds the BQL for the entire TB (translation block). A TB containing port I/O... TCG generates TBs and executes them; for I/O, it executes under the BQL (the whole TB execution happens under the BQL in QEMU 5.2's default TCG — yes, TCG holds the BQL for the entire TB execution!). If a guest TB is long (e.g., a 256-instruction TB with lots of port I/O), the BQL is held for that TB's execution time — on the order of microseconds. Not 60 seconds.\n\nBut — if the guest executes a TB that contains an infinite loop of port I/O without a branch boundary... no, TBs end at branches.\n\nOK, let me stop trying to fully understand the QMP instability. It's non-deterministic, and it usually recovers. My qmp.py has retries, but the retries reconnect... no wait, they don't — they just retry the same command over the same connection. Let me improve qmp.py: on command timeout, disconnect and reconnect (fresh state), then retry. This should handle the \"dirty state\" problem.\n\nAlso — importantly — the QMP instability may be caused by the guest's 60-second probe cycle hitting PCI/ISA ports and occasionally doing something slow. If I give the guest a working RTL8029 NIC, the probe succeeds quickly (no 60-second retry loop, no heavy port hammering), and the QMP instability may disappear entirely.\n\nSo let me build a custom QEMU with the RTL8029 PCI device. Plan:\n1. Check the QEMU 5.2.0 source tree at /tmp/qemu-5.2.0 (is it configured? is the build tree intact?).\n2. Create hw/net/rtl8029.c:\n   - A PCI device, vendor 0x10ec, device 0x8029, revision 0, class 0x02 (network), subclass 0x00 (Ethernet)\n   - BAR0: I/O space, 0x20 bytes, default 0x300\n   - IRQ pin A\n   - A 0x20-byte register area implementing NE2000-compatible behavior:\n     - Offset 0: Command (write-only; accepted values)\n     - Offset 1: Transceiver type (read/write; accepts 0x00, 0x12, 0x22, 0x32)\n     - Offset 2-5: DSA0-3 (read/write)\n     - Offset 6: ISR (read to read-and-clear bits; write to mask)\n     - Offset 7: RSR (read, low byte)\n     - Offset 8: RSR (read, high byte)\n     - Offset 9-13: Threshold registers (read/write)\n     - Offset 14-15: TSR (read, low/high)\n     - Offset 16+: Data port (read = receive data, write = transmit data)\n   - No actual network functionality needed (the driver just needs init to succeed; even a null backend is fine — though slirp integration would be nice; simplest: write-only data port is a no-op, read port returns 0, no received packets → ISR doesn't fire).\n\n   Actually, the simplest and most robust implementation: a register file where all read/write operations succeed with plausible values. The driver writes config, reads back, checks the transceiver type, etc. As long as:\n   - Write to transceiver register (base+1) with 0x22/0x32/0x12 → readback returns the same value ✓\n   - DSA registers are read/writable ✓\n   - Command register writes are accepted (readback is 0 or the written value) ✓\n   - No hang (no blocking operations) ✓\n   \n   ...then the driver's init will succeed.\n\n3. Register the device in hw/net/Kconfig + meson.build.\n4. Rebuild.\n5. Boot with `-device rtl8029,...` + `-netdev user`.\n\nLet me check the build tree first.\n\n\n\nThe QMP issue is non-deterministic, so I'll give the guest a working RTL8029 PCI device to avoid the retry loop. Now I'll check the QEMU 5.2.0 build tree.\n\n",
