ticking / the interrupt isn't being delivered despite IF=1).\n2. Something else is blocking.\n\nHmm. This is really deep. Let me step way back and think about the practical path to completion.\n\n**Practical reassessment:**\n\nI've spent a lot of effort on the RTL8029/NE2000 NIC issue. The fundamental problem: this Windows 3.1 image has a network driver (PCIND/RTL8029) that's required at boot, and it misbehaves with both \"no card\" (warning + infinite re-probe) and \"QEMU's ne2k_pci\" (initialization hangs in a buffer polling loop).\n\nI need to get the guest to the desktop. Let me reconsider the options:\n\n**Option A: Build a proper RTL8029 device in QEMU.**\nI have QEMU 5.2.0 source. I could create a new PCI device that identifies as 10ec:8029 and implements the RTL8029 register interface \"correctly\" (specifically the parts the driver polls). But I don't know exactly which register/behavior the driver is polling in the 0x3b5c loop. This is a lot of reverse engineering + QEMU development. High risk, high effort.\n\n**Option B: Modify the guest's configuration to skip the network driver.**\nThe driver is loaded from the disk (PROTOCOL.INI → PCIND.DOS). The disk is immutable (snapshot). I can't modify the base image. But wait — can I prevent the network stack from loading the driver via some non-disk mechanism?\n\nActually, let me reconsider. How is the network driver actually loaded during boot? In Windows 3.1 Workgroups, networking is loaded by:\n- AUTOEXEC.BAT calling a batch file, or\n- CONFIG.SYS, or\n- The Windows 3.1 \"network\" being enabled via PROTOCOL.INI, and WIN.COM/PROGMAN triggering it.\n\nThe actual loading of the NDIS driver (PCIND.DOS) is typically triggered by AUTOEXEC.BAT (e.g., a line like `net start` or loading of NDIS), or by a batch file in the WINDOWS directory.\n\nIf the loading is via AUTOEXEC.BAT or a batch/ini on disk, I can't modify it (immutable disk).\n\n**Option C: Reconsider — is the driver even necessary to skip in the first pla