      "thought": "EIP is stable at 0x3b5c over 5 seconds (5 samples, 1 second apart). So the guest is parked at EIP 0x3b5c — it's not running an instruction stream that moves the PC around much, it's parked at one address.\n\nBut earlier, single-stepping showed EIP toggling between 0x3b5c and 0x3b62. And the memory at 0x3b5c is all zeros.\n\nWait. If the memory at 0x3b5c is 00 00 00 00 and EIP stays at 0x3b5c, then the CPU is executing `add %al,(%eax)` (00 00) — no wait, that would advance EIP to 0x3b5d.\n\nUnless the CPU is HLT-ing at 0x3b5c! If the byte at 0x3b5c is 0x00... no, 0x00 is not HLT (HLT is 0xF4).\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-read the memory dump:\n```\n0x3b5c: 0x00000000 0x00000000 0x00000000 0x00000000\n0x3b6c: 0x00ff0000 0xff000000 0x0000ff00 0x00000000\n0x3b50: 0x00000000 0x000000ff 0x00000000 0x00000000\n0x3b60: 0x00000000 0x00000000 0x00000000 0x00ff0000\n```\nSo bytes at 0x3b5c-0x3b5f = 00 00 00 00. Bytes at 0x3b50-0x3b53 = 00 00 00 ff. So byte at 0x3b53 = 0xff.\n\nIf EIP=0x3b5c and the bytes are 00 00 00 00..., then the instruction is `add %al,(%eax)` (00 00), which would go to 0x3b5d. But EIP stays at 0x3b5c.\n\nThis is a contradiction. Unless GDB's EIP reading is wrong/stale, or the CPU is not actually executing at 0x3b5c.\n\nLet me reconsider. GDB connects to QEMU's GDB stub. The stub reports the CPU state. But maybe there's an issue with the GDB stub for this particular QEMU build, or the CPU is in a state where GDB misreads.\n\nActually — critical insight: maybe the guest is not executing at all. Maybe the CPU is in a HLT state, and GDB is reporting a stale EIP (0x3b5c) from before it halted. When the CPU HALTs, it stops executing, and EIP stays at the HLT instruction. GDB would report that stale EIP.\n\nBut the byte at 0x3b5c is 0x00, not HLT (0xF4). So if the CPU halted, it wouldn't have been at 0x3b5c.\n\nI'm clearly misunderstanding something about how GDB is reading this. Let me try a different approach: read EFLAGS and see if the CPU is in