ly).\n\nSo the original image was configured for a Cirrus Logic VGA card. I was using `-vga std` (standard/Bochs VGA), which is why the SVGA driver couldn't find its chip and fell back to CGA (the navy dialog). Now, with the NIC present, boot progressed and the SVGA driver tried to set up 1024x768 mode, but since the chip isn't actually Cirrus (it's std VGA), the mode setup produces a black screen.\n\n**The fix: use `-vga cirrus` instead of `-vga std`.**\n\nThis makes total sense! The image is configured for a Cirrus Logic card. Let me change the VGA to cirrus.\n\nBut wait — let me double-check. When I used `-vga std` without the NIC, I saw a CGA 4-color display with the navy \"No RTL8029\" dialog. That CGA fallback happened because the SVGA driver couldn't find a Cirrus chip. With `-vga cirrus`, the SVGA driver WOULD find the Cirrus chip and would use the proper SVGA mode from the start.\n\nSo I should:\n1. Change `-vga std` to `-vga cirrus` in start-vm.sh.\n2. Keep the ne2k_pci NIC.\n3. Restart and watch boot.\n\nWith a proper Cirrus VGA + RTL8029 NIC, the SVGA driver will initialize the display correctly (1024x768x256 or whatever mode 95 is), and Windows should boot to a proper desktop (not black, not CGA).\n\nLet me also reconsider the memory resolution: earlier with `-vga std` it was 720x400 (CGA). With cirrus, it should be a proper SVGA resolution.\n\nLet me update start-vm.sh to use `-vga cirrus`. Actually, let me reconsider whether to use cirrus or keep std. The PROTOCOL.INI explicitly says \"CirrusLogic 5436/46\". So cirrus is the right choice.\n\nLet me make the change and restart. First kill current QEMU, update the script, restart.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time whether `-vga std` in QEMU 5.2 is actually a Cirrus or a standard VGA. Let me check. In QEMU, the default VGA (and `-vga std`) is the \"i440fx PCI\" standard VGA, which emulates a Bochs/VGA 16-bit color card. `-vga cirrus` emulates the Cirrus Logic GD 5446. So yes, `-vga std` is NOT